  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nWe are given a step function h: [0,2] -> [0,1] with integral 1 that we wish to minimize the maximum correlation with its complement. The current best value is \u223c0.3809. Our strategy is to evolve a population of \"critical points\" that influence the maximum correlation by implementing a derivative-aware local search. \n\nWe move away from direct combinatorial optimization (flips of 0/1) to a physics-inspired approach that tracks \"action\" in the stationary measure of the correlation functional. By implementing a multi-modal annealing process with cooling schedules adapted to the functional landscape, we can better explore the space without getting trapped in shallow minima.\n\nKey innovations:\n- Introduce \"critical point\" variables that determine the functional value\n- Use a double-node network (saturated sigmoid + unit step) to model the discrete jumps\n- Geometric gradient descent over a \"minimal surface\" projection of the data\n\nThe algorithm dynamically forms \u201ccontributor segments\u201d that track the highest correlation regions and applies targeted annealing while maintaining effective connectivity between segments.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nVariables:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 \u2200i \u2208 \u03a9, \u03a9 = {1,...,N}\n- \u2211h[i] = N/2\n- Parameter vector \u03b1 = {\u03b11,...,\u03b1\u2098}\n\nObjective:\nC\u2085(\u03b1) = max\u2096 (2/\u221a(N\u00b3)) \u00b7 \u2211_{i} h[i](1-h[i+k])\n\nConstraints:\nh[i] = (1 - exp(-amp\u00b7w[i])) / (1 - exp(-amp)) for continuous segments\nh[i] = 0/1 for binary points\n\nSpecial formulation for the gradient:\n\u2207h C\u2085 = (c(k)/N\u2070) \u00b7 torch.autograd.grad(C\u2085, h) for susceptible points\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\nPhase initialization:\nInitialize population size P=40 with random binary vectors\nEvaluate C\u2085 for all P vectors\nAssociate energy to patterns based on correlation\n\nNetwork adaptation:\nConstruct bipartite graph between:\n  - Window size nodes (k from -N+1 to N-1)\n  - Measure nodes (for integrals of h(1-h+))\n\nCritical point selection:\nAt each iteration, choose 5% of highest-energy segments for \"magnification\"\nApply thermal perturbation with temperature T = (1-iteration/max_iters)*Initial_T\n\nAnnealing protocol:\nFor each critical point, apply local geometric transformations followed by projection:\n  h_new = exp(-\u2207f \u00b7 \u03c3\u00b2) \u2297 h_old \u2295 (higher curvature adjustment)\n\nConstraint enforcement:\nUse minimal-energy relaxation for integral constraint:\n  Adjust single-node position by force = \u03bb * (sum constraint - error) with \u03bb=100/||gradient||\n\nPhase transition detection between high/low curvature regions with statistical discriminant D.\n\n4. Search choices\n\nDefault parameterization:\n- Minimal temperature T_min=0.01\n- Initial temperature T=0.3\n- Annealing rate \u03c4=0.995\n- Transition frequency \u03b4=0.3\n- Population divergence factor \u03b1=0.8\n\nMutation schemes:\na. Magnification waves (heavy breathing)\nb. Focused overheating (local voids)\nc. Global washout protocols\n\nImplementation of local transformations using symplectic integrators:\n\n  p[i] = p[i] * (1 + \u03b7 * sign(-driving field))\n  while_norm(p) do: normalize by (1/5) divergence factor\n\n5. Compute budget\n\nProcessing stages:\n- Initial evaluation (n): O(NP) where N\u22481300 points, P=40 -> ~52k operations\n- Cooldown phases: Spent distributed across 300 iterations (each iteration: O(N) + O(P))\n- Network update: O(mN) where m=30 transition vectors\n\nResources required:\n1 CPU core with 32GB RAM, 150us per convolution, 423ms overhead for projection\n\nReadiness checks during execution:\n- Relative energy improvement < 0.001 -> early transition to annealing \n- Network divergence exceeding threshold -> adaptive reevaluation\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\nFeasibility checks:\n1. ||h|| = N/2 \u00b1 0.5%\n2. All values in [0,1]\n3. Score computed by:\n   score = (max_k integrate|h(1-h+)|) / (2/N/\u221a(N\u00b3))\n\nSanity checks:\n- Secondary heat capacity validation\n- Stationarity indexes (symplectic invariants)\n- Consistency between binary and continuous representations\n\nRetrieval protocol:\n- Restore best solution if network degradation above 3%\n- Measure performance by (initial_upper_bound - final_score)/initial_upper_bound\n\nOutput structure:\n- h_values: np.array of length N\n- c5_bound: float32 score\n- n_points: integer (chosen grid size)\n\nNetwork rejuvenation signal:\nWhen multiple local optima detected (frequency > 0.45), perform global reconfiguration with random sample perturbation",
